to-victor-taelin-source : List Word
to-victor-taelin-source = lines-of (cons "# Victor Taelin"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Victor, you took Lafont's interaction combinators seriously as a machine, at a time when the field treated optimal reduction as a curiosity in a few papers by Lamping, Asperti and Guerrini. You built a lineage on it: the abstract algorithm (2017), the Interaction Calculus, HVM, HVM2 running interaction nets on GPUs, Bend, and Kind. Every piece of your work that hyperactivity builds on is yours, with full credit. Each claim below about Avik Jain's work links to the block of his paper, *hyperactivity*, Part I, that states it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What you saw that the field did not"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The field treats evaluation as sequential, with parallelism bolted on through threads, locks and schedulers, and sharing bolted on through caches. Your work showed that both belong to the calculus. Interaction combinators rewrite locally and are strongly confluent: every redex can fire in any order, at the same time, with the same result. So parallelism is a fact about the program, which the hardware either uses or wastes. Optimal sharing is a fact about the program too: a duplicated lambda does not duplicate the work inside it. You made that run on real hardware and defended it in public for a decade."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Bend 2 (released 17 September 2026) carries the same instinct to the other end. Code written by an AI is untrusted; the human states the laws, the type checker checks the proofs, and the build refuses any change it cannot prove still obeys them. Correctness comes from the proof, not from tests."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity takes from it"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The paper places sharing exactly where you do. Sharing is a property of a represented computation: two occurrences refer to one producer, a dependent consumer requests its result, and graph-based local reduction has an established account in interaction nets"
  (cons (cite s6-5-p2 "(§6.5).")
  (cons "Sharing changes the graph of dependencies and does not change the typing, substitution or equality judgments of the terms"
  (cons (cite s6-5-p2 "(§6.5).")
  (cons "An interpreter is already a program, and one evaluator realized natively runs every represented term, under stated premises of evaluator adequacy and machine simulation"
  (cons (cite s9-2-p2 "(§9.2).")
  (cons ""
  (cons "Cubical operations are computation, not side conditions. Cubical substitution replaces dimension variables with interval expressions, and composition determines the value needed to make a boundary coherent, so equality is something the evaluator acts on"
  (cons (cite s3-1-p6 "(§3.1).")
  (cons "Those operations are local to the structure they act on, which is why they suit a substrate like yours."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Univalence, and why it is cubical"
  (cons ""
  (cons "You have said you want univalence, and that you want it “(NOT cubical).” Here is the case that the two are one request."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Univalence says equivalent types are equal, so what is proven about one carries over to the other. Postulated as an axiom, it does not compute: transport along it gets stuck. In a cubical presentation the path between equivalent types is constructed with Glue types, and transport along it computes the equivalence's forward action"
  (cons (cite s4-p5 "(§4),")
  (cons "with the univalence computation law as a path whose judgmental behavior is fixed by the chosen cubical rules"
  (cons (cite s4-p7 "(§4).")
  (cons "Huber proved canonicity for cubical type theory, and Cubical Agda made it a working language."
  (cons ""
  (cons "In hyperactivity this has consequences for your own goals. An equivalence of representations gives a path between the representation types, and a dependent predicate, or an operation with its proof, is transported along it"
  (cons (cite s4-p14 "(§4).")
  (cons "A specialization comes with a path to the general program"
  (cons (cite s6-2-p6 "(§6.2).")
  (cons "Content addressing identifies a stored representation, a different statement from equality of meaning, and the store records each relation with its evidence"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p5 "(§10.8).")
  (cons ""
  (cons "## The history, stated plainly"
  (cons ""
  (cons "In Avik's words: “Taelin specifically rejected the idea of using cubical which I raised (without any curiosity into my statements about the resulting capacities) in his Bend language which I'd forked and developed cubical calculus in, later posting he wants univalence “(NOT cubical)” leading me to do hyperactivity the right way on my own.”"
  (cons ""
  (cons "That is the record, and it is not a grievance. You built the substrate. He brought the theory to it, and when it found no home in Bend, he built the whole system himself. What he offers now is what you said you wanted: univalence that computes, on the kind of machine you pioneered."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Credit"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The interaction-combinator machine, HVM's realization of optimal sharing on parallel hardware, the Interaction Calculus and Bend's proof-gated workflow are your work. Lafont, Lamping and Asperti hold theirs. Read this as work done on your foundations, because it is."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- HVM2 paper, https://docs.rs/crate/hvm/latest/source/paper/PAPER.typst"
  (cons "- The Interaction Calculus, https://github.com/VictorTaelin/Interaction-Calculus"
  (cons "- The abstract algorithm, https://github.com/VictorTaelin/abstract-algorithm"
  (cons "- Bend 2 release coverage, Akita on Rails, 19 September 2026, https://akitaonrails.com/en/2026/09/19/new-ai-language-just-released-bend-2/"
  (cons "- Cohen, Coquand, Huber, Mörtberg, Cubical Type Theory, https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.02108"
  (cons "- Huber, Canonicity for cubical type theory, https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.04156"
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, Part I, the blocks linked above"
  (cons "- Taelin's “(NOT cubical)” post is cited from Avik's account; it was not found in the searches made for this page."
  nil))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
